WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
Mayuko Inoue: Hey everyone, how's it going? My name is
Mayuko and welcome back to my channel.

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.500
So when I was getting my very first software engineering job, I had
no idea how the recruiting process works.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.409
Like I was just trying to get that first job.

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:15.028
And the whole
process seemed like a black box that I couldn't see through whatsoever.

00:00:15.028 --> 00:00:19.375
Once I entered the industry. However, I started helping out with the recruiting stuff,

00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:23.076
like going to events and sitting on interview
panels and stuff like that.

00:00:23.076 --> 00:00:27.143
And it started just to make a lot more sense to me like how all of it worked.

00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:36.585
And honestly, after I was able to see how things worked from the inside, I feel like ultimately, for every job
after that I was just a much better candidate because I knew how things worked.

00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:41.643
So I kind of figured it would be really useful for a lot of y'all who are trying to get that first job as software engineers,

00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:44.720
to learn
about how the recruiting process works.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.102
So I invited my friend Sarah, who's lead negotiator at level.fyi,

00:00:47.102 --> 00:00:54.775
and who also worked as a recruiter for multiple FAANG companies to my show Muko's Corner to talk about the
recruiting process.

00:00:54.775 --> 00:01:01.798
I've had her on the show before and we've talked about salary negotiation, but just her experiences are so valuable because

00:01:01.798 --> 00:01:07.185
she really knows the ins and outs of recruiting and the do's and don'ts of getting that first job.

00:01:07.185 --> 00:01:14.198
So in today's video, I'm going to share with you highlights from that show all
about how the recruiting process works.

00:01:14.198 --> 00:01:21.287
Everything from when the recruiting season starts to where companies decide to recruit candidates from and more.

00:01:21.287 --> 00:01:22.967
So let's get started.

00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:29.130
What does a typical process look like for
a new grad, to get a job out of college into the tech industry?

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:35.700
Sarah: In terms of like from a candidate perspective is
going to be pretty similar to an industry recruiting,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.460
where you're going to go through the process of talking
to your recruiter, and then the first technical phone

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:47.310
interview. And then the final round. So usually, for
some company, they like to kind of shrink it and only

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:53.280
have you talk to maybe two to three software engineers,
and then some company would want you to talk to three

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:00.120
to five engineers. Now these interviewers are usually
those that just volunteer to be an interviewer. So

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:06.720
these interviewers that you talk to from a new grad
perspective or intern, they may not be the people that

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:14.970
you will be working with. In terms of like the process,
it tend to be a little bit more volume driven. So for

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:20.220
industry recruiting, usually what we look for is
something very specific, like we need a backend

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:26.940
engineer who have experience with cloud computing, for
example, versus for intern or new grad, we usually just

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.220
call them as like fungible engineer, let's just hire
them through and then we'll figure out a team for them

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:38.850
after. So a lot of it is usually by cohort too. So a
lot of it, it's almost like I mean, the best way to

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.070
describe it is like batch hiring.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:48.420
Mayuko Inoue: When does like recruiting start for new
grads? I guess. So if someone was to graduate in 2022,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.980
let's say what's the earliest that companies are going
to start looking to hire new grads?

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:58.930
Sarah: Yeah, good question. So for new grad an intern,
the process start a lot sooner than when like they

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:06.520
actually open up the job. So usually for Amazon, we do
operational planning. So we do operational planning one

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.290
and then operational planning two. So there's going to
be like two times when we will reveal like headcount

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:18.130
and things like that. And budget, what does that look
like, and only certain team choose to participate in,

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:23.410
okay, we want to have an intern and some teams can say,
Hey, we don't have that capacity right now and

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:30.010
therefore, we're not going to participate. And so for
new grads, usually the hiring process already start in

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:36.550
January of the year and the headcount, they usually get
headcount from August of the previous years already,

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:43.330
the process, the headcount planning, all of that
happens a lot sooner than you expected. And so usually

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:49.780
around this time, and it sucks for a lot of folks who
graduate in December, when they start applying, and

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:55.000
they don't see anything, and they just start to wonder,
like, Is there something wrong with my resume? What do

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.160
I need to do to kind of optimize my chance and
sometimes too like, the headcount doesn't open until

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:06.550
March or June, and then the interview process will
happen about like one to two months, and then you get

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:10.300
your offer and then you start in like, around like
August.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:17.140
Mayuko Inoue: So is it right then to kind of generalize
that, like each company kind of has like, a number like

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:22.990
they have, like, there's this many seats open for new
grads. And then so like you said, like new grad,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:28.480
recruiting tends to be very volume based, where it's
literally just like, interview as many people as you

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.670
can from different schools and try to fill those seats.
Is that kind of like a good summary of how that works?

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:39.160
Sarah: Yeah, so there's like internship, and there's
also co-op. So for some company like Microsoft, for

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:46.720
example. Or even like Facebook, they have specific
recruiter to support certain school. And then some

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.600
company like for example, Apple can choose to have a
much longer internship or that's what called  co-op,

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:58.420
where it can be anywhere from six months to a year. And
then there's only some company that say, you know, we

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:06.190
don't want to do that. We only want to do like, you
know, 12 weeks sort of like program for intern. And

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:13.660
usually I would say like, stay on top of that company
internship program. Because when we post a job, we just

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:19.420
get so many applicants. So usually the job only stay up
for about a month. And then after that, we're going to

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:24.730
take them down to process all of the applicants that we
have from those pipelines.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:30.550
Mayuko Inoue: How do companies decide where to recruit
from, as far as like choosing which universities?

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:35.380
Sarah: Yeah, so usually, they would choose like top
tech companies. So for example, at Microsoft, they

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:41.980
would have a team of recruiter who only go after an Ivy
League school, and then we have, Microsoft sometime

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:49.270
would have like a team that go to like top tech school
like UC Berkeley or Waterloo. And you know, it really

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:55.900
depends, and usually company pays so much money to
these schools. And that's where they want that ROI back

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:56.890
in their pocket.

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:02.200
Mayuko Inoue: Yeah, that makes sense it like I think I
get a lot of questions about like, is it worth it to go

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:07.720
to a four year university for computer science? Or
should I go to a boot camp? And ultimately, one of the

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:12.250
things that I think is important to think about is
like, what kind of connections does that school have?

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:16.510
Because if you go to a school that has those
connections, like if you go to UW, it's going to have a

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:20.830
connection to Microsoft, because it's right there. If
you go to Stanford, you're gonna have connections to

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:28.000
basically every tech company there is. And so like,
it's not just about I think, the education or the

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.590
experience of going to school, but it's the network
that you gain as a part of being able to go to that

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:39.310
schools. How does it work on the recruiting side that
like we just general people, normal people who are

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:43.930
trying to get a job into tech that we don't know about?
Like, what's something that you think is like, a piece

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.070
of information that recruiters might all know, but
yeah, it's it's not common knowledge out there.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:52.770
Sarah: There's just a lot of different channel for like
sourcing candidates. So sometimes we don't just look

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:58.320
through like the people that apply. And sometimes it
will take us to kind of go out there and operate almost

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:04.470
like a headhunter like to see like who's doing what and
you know, which in turn are new grad would be good for

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:10.350
certain position like this is more common for machine
learning and AI just because usually, there's something

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:16.860
specific that we're looking for. The part that's I
think that can can be a little bit tricky to navigate

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:23.490
here is industry recruiting is completely different
than new grad recruiting. And they're just a lot more

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:30.450
red tape being put around new grad and internship. So
sometimes, like, the difficult part is that we, I just

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:36.180
get so many messages from students, you know, asking
for, if they can take a look at my resume and things

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:41.250
like that. And this, I can't do anything about it, I
don't support the campus. And I don't have any

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:49.110
visibility into that. Even for an Amazon, we have two
completely applicant tracking system where one is, or

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:55.140
only intern. And unless if I support intern, that's the
only way that I would get access into it. So I can't

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:04.620
just randomly submit someone else and resume for that
pod. And that's something that kind of like sucks at

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:11.040
the same time. Like as much as I want to help. This
isn't anything that I can do. So for those that you

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.830
know, maybe want to reach out to your recruiter be
strategic about who you are reaching out to. So instead

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:22.080
of just going on LinkedIn, and just look for a like
technical recruiter and send your resume that way,

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:27.930
don't do that, do a little bit more of a tailor search,
search for campus recruiter, search for a co op

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:33.030
recruiter or search for a like new grad recruiter,
because you're probably will hear back from those

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:37.950
recruiter, more than like a leadership or like an
industry recruiter.

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:42.400
Mayuko Inoue: I think there's a lot of pressure to have
like a computer science degree to get that software

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:48.730
engineering job. most universities, computer science is
an impacted major, which means it's really, really hard

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:52.930
to get into their programs and really, really hard to
stay in the program. So there's just a lot of pressure

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:59.740
to like, stay a computer science student. And I think
there is a lot of just like this notion that if you

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:06.190
don't graduate with a degree, then you won't be able to
get that software engineering role. But what do you

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:11.680
think about that from a campus recruiting perspective?
Is it really important for there to be a line that says

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:13.510
B.S. computer science?

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:20.560
Sarah: Yeah, so I'm gonna get the PhD out of the way
first, because PhD is something very specific and

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:26.470
usually they will require you know, that you have a
computer science or whatever it is that the company

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:33.160
really needs. So, PhD read your job description, it
will say as a requirement then therefore, you will need

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:40.330
a computer science in order to get that PhD internship
or Scholar Program or full time position. But for

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:46.270
bachelor and master degree, if you read the job
description carefully for The education section, they

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:53.500
don't say you are required to have a computer science
usually a lot of company will say computer science or

00:09:53.500 --> 00:10:02.980
stem or related field and when they say that a computer
science degree is not required. And actually, in fact,

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:10.780
like from I mean, from my time working at Amazon and a
few other companies, the best engineer that we have

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:17.500
actually didn't have a computer science degree. The
best engineer that we have came from degree like math,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:25.600
especially math, physics, and some even came from more
like education background or even like music and those

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:31.360
make really good software engineer. So you don't need a
computer science unless if you're wanting to do

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:36.760
something very specific, like machine learning and AI,
then, you know, the company can require that. But for a

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:38.650
bachelor and master, the answer is no.

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:45.820
Mayuko Inoue: Yeah, the math, physics, education, and
music majors, it's just that like boggles my mind,

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:50.020
because that's not like when I was going to school, I
was like, that's not what I want to major on major

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:55.300
computer only way to get a job. But that's, that's
really interesting from a campus recruiting

00:10:55.330 --> 00:11:01.660
perspective, like who's the one to make the decision of
hire versus no hire if it's a little bit like more

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:03.940
abstracted away from the teams?

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:09.540
Sarah: Yeah, so this is kind of like where it gets
really tricky, because the manager that you talk to

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:14.850
that person may not be your manager when you start that
position. So usually, I mean, I'm speaking from like

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:21.750
Amazon, and I specifically support Amazon Web Services,
we would just kind of put the words out there in terms

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:28.290
of like, hey, in recruiting, we're trying to put
together a team of, you know, interviewers, like who

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:33.810
would want to participate, so only certain manager
would say, okay, like, I'm open to do this, and then

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:39.240
only certain engineer that want to be a part of it. The
nice thing about Amazon is that if you're trying to

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:45.270
become a bar raiser, it does help kind of get your
interview count in. Because at Amazon to be a bar

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:50.670
raiser, you need like 100 interviews to become one. So
like, sometimes like for the people that want to get

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:59.550
promoted, they would like want to volunteer to do the
interview, in terms of like making a yes or no hire

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:06.300
like decision, each person on the interview panel would
have like a rubric. So let's say if there's three

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:12.060
interviewers on your panel, one person would cover data
structure and algorithm, one person would cover object

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:17.160
oriented design, and then maybe the manager would be
the one that do like cultural fit. And there would be

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:22.020
like, the checklists that they go through that the
candidate do this. If they do, let's say, let's say

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:28.320
this candidate checks, 7 boxes out of 10. That's a
maybe and if the candidate only check 1 out of 10,

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:35.640
okay, that's a hard No, or the candidate check 8 out of
10 boxes. Okay, that's a yes. And then usually, we

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:41.520
would have a debrief to where all of the interviewers
sit in a room kind of talk about, you know, that

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:46.560
candidate performance and whether or not they would be
a good fit for the program or the company.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:52.230
Mayuko Inoue: That's so fascinating. That's like
totally separate from how I thought campus recruiting

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:57.210
would happen. It's literally like, it's very rubric
focus. And it seems it's just like some you're just

00:12:57.210 --> 00:13:03.750
trying to find like a well rounded engineer who has
like computer science fundamentals. Sarah can new grads

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.180
negotiate salaries, when they get their offer?

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:13.200
Sarah: For new grads usually is going to be really hard
because everything is again, we're operating from

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:18.840
volume here. And usually by cohort, just because of you
know, pay quality, we want to pay everyone the same.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:25.110
For a co-op, usually, there's a little bit more of like
flexibility in terms of negotiating but not much. But

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:31.770
as a new grad, you do have the power to negotiate. In
fact, for some company like Microsoft, they want to

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:38.160
reward you for you know, going back to them. So if you
had a previous internship with them, they will give you

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:41.610
a higher compensation package when you come back to the
company.

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:45.620
Mayuko Inoue: Thank you for sharing that. Thank you all
so much for watching until the very end, I hope it

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:51.110
helped you to better understand how the recruiting
process works. Sarah, of course, as always shared so

